{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-36108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,1,0)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,1,0)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+yz+{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+(1)(0)+(0)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(1)+(-1)(0)-(1)(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0+0}{1+1+0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-36108" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-36108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-36108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-36108" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129322" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129322" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129329" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\sqrt[3]{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\sqrt[3]{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129329" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"] ["math" "e"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74909" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74952" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74952" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Unfortunately, this limit takes the indeterminate form because the numerator and denominator both evaluate to zero at the given point:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)^{2}}{1\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot (-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+xy-{y}^{2}}{z{x}^{2}-z{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+2xy-xy-{y}^{2}}{z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x(x+y)-y(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+y)}{z(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y,z)\\to (1,-1,1)}\\frac{2x-y}{z(x-y)} & =\\frac{2(1)-(-1)}{1(1-(-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-35956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35956" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}-3({2}^{2})(2)+3(2)({2}^{2})-{2}^{3}}{{2}^{2}-2(2)(2)+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-24+24-8}{4-8+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)^{3}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)(x-y)^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)\\cancel{(x-y)^{2}}}{\\cancel{(x-y)^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,2)}(x-y) & =2-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74957" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "\\text{DNE}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74957" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\frac{1+0-{1}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{(\\sqrt{x+y}-z)(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{x+y-{z}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x+y-{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{\\cancel{x+y-{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z) & =\\sqrt{1+0}+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129346" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129346" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & =\\frac{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}-1}{({1}^{2})(2+(-1))-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+z}-1}{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{(x\\sqrt{y+z}-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}(y+z)-1}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}(y+z)-1)}(x\\sqrt{y+z}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,2,-1)}\\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{y+z}+1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\sqrt{2+(-1)}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129345" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74960" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-74960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74960" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit does not exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0-0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x)-x(x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(-x)-x(-x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(-x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-35953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-35953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$\n- The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit does not exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$\n- The limit does not exist" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36244cac989010d41dc9eef69c0dbd30372046daf212f7acfa4b431e5fc0d141"} {"question" "q-129408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-129408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d3b293274a857a6868f852277a4a37b1aecb00bfbd975c31eac455b8d8"} {"question" "q-74966" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{3}}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/state.json" "file_sha256" "8df850d