"value" "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-3330.json" "file_sha256" "130018fa1d3687c7d2ca209ff144efddd8e87a1d8219051e507146f5f5aaae74"} {"question" "e-3331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-3331.json" "file_sha256" "592eaff0058f7adaa8dac27bfe1ed14876d4c065e5e2a3d645eafcd181fb1dc1"} {"question" "e-3331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-3331.json" "file_sha256" "592eaff0058f7adaa8dac27bfe1ed14876d4c065e5e2a3d645eafcd181fb1dc1"} {"question" "e-6239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-6239.json" "file_sha256" "5d09544c0a672cc6ed38e0c01e8a5649a4e9015c4b6f1e4be0affa5bfec90d8e"} {"question" "e-6239" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-6239.json" "file_sha256" "5d09544c0a672cc6ed38e0c01e8a5649a4e9015c4b6f1e4be0affa5bfec90d8e"} {"question" "e-3366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-3366.json" "file_sha256" "b37b8e73a10e9f09ce59ced048667df3fc91c28a4928bb7eb3e34057c8bcd5a7"} {"question" "e-3366" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042465/example-3366.json" "file_sha256" "b37b8e73a10e9f09ce59ced048667df3fc91c28a4928bb7eb3e34057c8bcd5a7"}] "usage" {"e-3331" [] "e-3366" [] "q-35953" [] "e-3330" [] "e-6239" []} "basis" 1553 "retractions" [[17592186124854 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n$lim_(x → (0, 0))\\frac{xy - xy^{2}}{x^{2} + 2xy}$\n\n1. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y = x$\n2. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y =-x$\n3. The limit exists and is equal to $1$"] [17592186124171 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}$."] [17592186124171 :question/worked-solution "The function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 2))\\frac{xy^{3}}{x + y} &= ((-1) \\cdot 2^{3})/((-1) + 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{1} \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124172 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}$."] [17592186124172 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 1))\\frac{x^{2}y + xy^{2}}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= lim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy(x + y))/((x + y)(x - y)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy)/((x - y))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy)/((x - y)) &= ((-1) \\cdot 1)/(-1 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186124285 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}$."] [17592186124285 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (2, 1))\\frac{x^{2} - 4y}{x - 2\\sqrt{y}} &= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/((x - 2\\sqrt{y})(x + 2\\sqrt{y})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/(x^{2} - (2\\sqrt{y})^{2}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/(x^{2} - 4y) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))(x + 2\\sqrt{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (2, 1))(x + 2\\sqrt{y}) &= 2 + 2\\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4$."] [17592186124176 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}$."] [17592186124176 :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y = x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2x))/(x^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{2}}; = 3$.\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y =-x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2(-x)))/((-x)^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{-x^{2}}{x^{2}}; =-1$.\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122147 17592186122148 175